 #Sender Spoofing
•	The sender uses a domain designed to look like PayPal, replacing “l” with “I”(one).
•	Indicates impersonation and domain spoofing.
 #Header Discrepancies
•	Return-Path does not match the From address.
•	Received IP registered overseas—outside of legitimate mail server ranges.
•	Suggests tampering or unauthorized relay.
#Suspicious Links
•	Link text: “Verify Your Account”
•	Hover URL points to login.paypaI-secure-auth.com, a fake domain.
•	Classic indicator of credential-harvesting intent.
#Suspicious Attachment
•	HTML file (“Account_Verification.html”) included.
•	HTML attachments often host phishing login forms.
 #Urgency & Fear Tactics
•	Claims of “permanent suspension” and “immediate action required.”
•	Designed to create panic and reduce user judgment.
#Spelling & Grammar Errors
•	Multiple mistakes and inconsistent formatting.
•	Common trait of phishing campaigns.
 #Branding Issues
•	Distorted logo and missing official footer links.
•	Indicates template misuse or poor imitation.

